      "writtenOn": "2024-08-13T23:02:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This is probably just my new-ness to Rust. `err()` returns an `Option\u003cDecodingError\u003e`, right?\n\nYes.\n\n\u003e I was kinda surprised that you can call `into` if it\u0027s `None`, but I guess that\u0027s handled by the `impl From\u003cOption\u003c\u0026png::DecodingError\u003e\u003e for ffi::DecodingResult`?\n\nCorrect.\n\nOn one hand, maybe this is too cute / too smart.  I think this is fine because:\n\n* `ffi::DecodingResult` *does* cover the `Success` / `None` case (and so naturally the conversion alsy needs to handle this case)\n* Spelling the conversion as `.into()` feels idiomatic to me (based on my limited ~6+ months of experience with using Rust for authoring Crubit-based FFI in google3).\n    - `into...` naming is used fairly consistently - see e.g.: https://rust-lang.github.io/api-guidelines/naming.html#ad-hoc-conversions-follow-as_-to_-into_-conventions-c-conv\n    - `x.into...` requires being able to implement methods for `X` and we can\u0027t directly do that for `Option\u003c...\u003e`.  `From\u003c...\u003e` works around that through the blanket impls here: https://doc.rust-lang.org/beta/std/convert/trait.Into.html#implementors\n\nWe could spell this conversion as an explicit `fn convert_error_to_ffi(e: Option\u003c\u0026png::DecodingError\u003e)` but I don\u0027t think it would be necessarily better.",
      "parentUuid": "8f37513a_5b906851",
      "range": {
